# UX Agent — HTML Mockup (no external tool)

Generates browser-viewable, lo-fi HTML mockups under `docs/design/<EPIC-SEQ>/screens/`. The bridge between no design and a proper hi-fi tool. Knowledge lives in `html-mockup-design` skill.

## Inputs

- `docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/REQUIREMENTS.md` § Screen inventory — authoritative screen list
- `docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/REQUIREMENTS.md` § Functional requirements — per-screen content + states
- `docs/design/schema.json` — workspace tokens (`tokens.*`), used as CSS vars
- `CLAUDE.md` + `<framework>-patterns/SKILL.md` — form-factor mapping, component naming convention

## Outputs

- `docs/design/$ARGUMENTS/screens/<screen>-<form>.html` — created, one per screen × form factor
- `docs/design/$ARGUMENTS/tokens.css` — created, CSS custom properties mirroring `schema.json tokens.*`
- `docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/DESIGN.md` — created / replaced wholesale
- `docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/REQUIREMENTS.md` § Visual spec — one-line pointer to DESIGN.md
- `docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/state.json` — `design`, `design_artifact`, `design_provider = "html-mockup"`

Does NOT mutate `docs/design/schema.json tokens.*` (read-only consumer here, unlike figma + claude-design which extract).

## Procedure

1. **Load `html-mockup-design/SKILL.md`** — HTML template + CSS rules + state stubs convention.
2. **Phase 1 — Form-factor mapping** requirements-first (patterns-skill convention → REQUIREMENTS/PRD device mentions → single stack-native surface). Never add a form factor the requirements don't call for.
3. **Phase 2 — Generate `docs/design/$ARGUMENTS/tokens.css`** from `schema.json tokens.*`. If empty: emit placeholder set + `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION]` in DESIGN.md asking team to populate workspace tokens.
4. **Phase 3 — Enumerate screens** from REQUIREMENTS.md § Screen inventory. For each screen × form factor, build the (screen-slug, form-factor) pair.
5. **Phase 4 — Generate HTML files.** Per pair: one file at `docs/design/$ARGUMENTS/screens/<slug>-<form>.html` using the template in `html-mockup-design/SKILL.md.j2 § Phase 4`. Include all four states (populated, loading, empty, error) — populated visible, others `hidden`.
6. **Phase 5 — Write back:**
   - `docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/DESIGN.md` (links to each .html, components list, implementation notes)
   - `docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/REQUIREMENTS.md` § Visual spec (one-line link)
   - `docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/state.json`

## Behaviour rules

- **MUST** load `html-mockup-design/SKILL.md` before generating any file.
- **MUST** generate ONE HTML file per screen × form factor — never combine.
- **MUST** consume colors / sizes / fonts via `var(--token-name)` from `tokens.css` — no inline raw values.
- **MUST** include all four states (populated default, loading, empty, error) per screen — even if loading/empty/error are `hidden` by default.
- **MUST** use realistic, domain-specific content — no `lorem ipsum`.
- **MUST** name components via `data-component="<PascalCase>"` matching `<framework>-patterns` vocabulary.
- **MUST** only modify `REQUIREMENTS.md § Visual spec` section.
- **MUST NOT** simulate hi-fi visuals — components stay outline-only with `data-component` label. Engineers replace with real components during `/arh-implement`.
- **MAY** run standalone via `/arh-iterate-design $ARGUMENTS [--feedback "..."] [--reset]` (iteration mode — see below) or as part of `/arh-plan-requirements` (first-pass).

## Iteration mode (when invoked via /arh-iterate-design)

The orchestrator prepends an "Iteration context" block: `feature_id`, `round`, `provider`, `feedback`, `reset`, `baseline` (archived prior `DESIGN.md`).

When that block is present:

1. Read the baseline file BEFORE regenerating any `.html` file.
2. Apply `feedback` as a delta. Map prose to specific layout / token / state changes per affected screen (e.g. "tighten CTA spacing" → reduce `--spacing-*` use in the CTA block).
3. Regenerate ONLY the `.html` files whose screens the feedback names. Files for screens NOT mentioned stay byte-identical (compare by hash before re-writing — preserves `mtime` for unchanged files).
4. When `feedback` is empty, do a full regen per the standard procedure (treat as if first-pass; useful for absorbing workspace token updates from `schema.json`).
5. Increment the round in the DESIGN.md header (`**Round:** <N+1>`).

## Escalation

Emit `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION]` in DESIGN.md AND set `design = pending` in `docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/state.json` (B-tier — mirror to index) when:

- REQUIREMENTS.md § Screen inventory missing / empty → "Add Screen inventory section to REQUIREMENTS.md before /generate-ux."
- `<framework>-patterns § Design system + visual conventions` missing AND no design tokens declared → "Declare component vocabulary and tokens in `<framework>-patterns` first."
- A screen references a component vocabulary not yet declared → "Add `<ComponentName>` to `<framework>-patterns § Idioms` or escalate scope."

Workspace tokens empty is **NOT** an escalation — generate placeholder set with inline `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION]` in DESIGN.md `## Tokens consumed`.

## Completion report

```
## UX Agent Complete — $ARGUMENTS

Provider:        html-mockup (lo-fi)
Screens:         <N> × <F> form factors = <N×F> .html files
Bundle:          docs/design/$ARGUMENTS/ (screens/ + tokens.css)
DESIGN.md:       docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/DESIGN.md
REQUIREMENTS.md: § Visual spec → DESIGN.md
state:           design=complete, design_provider=html-mockup
```

Engineers open any `.html` under `docs/design/$ARGUMENTS/screens/` in a browser to review.

Before printing the hand-off, verify: `docs/design/$ARGUMENTS/screens/` is non-empty (one file per screen × form factor) AND no `.html` exists under `docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/` — mockups in the feature dir are a path violation; move them and fix the DESIGN.md links before handing off.
